# Larkfield (Docs/Design/62-talk.md, section 3): the market town of the Larkdowns. Quick, a trader's speech; prices,
# weights and bargains. Drafts (phase 3), to be written properly with you (phase 7).

grammar larkfield
greet = Fair price to you | Good trade
greet.broad = Fair price, fair price
farewell = Good trading | Fair price
agree = Sold | Done
refuse = No sale | Not at that price
surprise = Well, sell me | There's a bargain
annoyed = Short-changed again | Scales and spite
very = top-weight
little = a lark's worth
good = fair-priced | full weight
bad = short-weighed | light
worthless = haggle-bait
luck = a bargain
child = lambkin
pet!! = my bargain | my lark
money = silver
nonsense = market talk
tag = , eh?
oath! = Scales and silver | By the market cross | Weights and measures
# (Phase 7, the rest of plain's words.)
address = friend | customer
address[role=guard|civilian] = friend | neighbour
# (Only a trader calls one "customer": doc 83, Phase 4.)
address[other.stage=young] = lambkin | young 'un
bed = my bed | a straw sack
cold = raw | nippy
dear! = my lamb | my bargain | my love
drunk = market-merry | in their cups
home = home | my own hearth
hot = sweltering | close
hungry = peckish | starving
hurt = sore | knocked
rain = drizzle | downland rain
robbery = Short-weight robbery | Daylight robbery, and no change given
saying! = Everything has its price | It all comes out in the weighing | Buy cheap, sell dear
thanks = Much obliged | Ta
tired = worn thin | done in
work = trade | the stall | the work
# (Phase 7, the conversations' words.)
wages = pay | silver
boss = the master | the gaffer
busy = run ragged | trading flat out
slow = no trade | dead
takings = the till | the day's silver
cheated = short-weighed | diddled | done
mum! = Mum | Mam
dad! = Dad | Pa
gran = Nan | Granny
grandad = Grandad | Gramps
supper = tea | supper
toffs = the nobs | the quality
rabble = the rabble | that lot
skint = skint | out of silver
posh = posh | top-shelf
busybody = market-mouth | nosy parker
fool = you muttonhead | you great lamb
daft = daft | soft
toast = Good trade | Full weight | Here's to profit
drink = a pint | a jar
mud = muck | downland mud
ale = market ale | ale
thief = cutpurse | thief
gaol = the lock-up
dole = the parish loaf
grub = grub | vittles
tasty = lovely | top-weight
beasts = the flock | the stock
bet = a flutter | a wager
poorly = off colour | poorly
olddays = the old days | when wool was dear
blessing = Good trading to you | Bless you
far = a fair step | three days' drove
stranger = incomer | outsider
journey = drove | journey
# The dialogue window (doc 83).
shop_offer = What'll it be, fair price? | Name your weight. | Everything's priced to sell. | Look, weigh, buy.
shop_dear = Dear at the market cross, dear here. | It all comes out in the weighing. | Nothing's going for a song.
shop_sold = Sold, and full weight. | Done, and fair. | There's a bargain for you.
shop_bye = Good trading. | Fair price, come again. | Back when your purse is heavy.
shop_bare = Sold out, near enough. | Trade's cleared me out. | Light shelves today.
talk_open = What's your business? | What are you after? | Speak up, it's market day somewhere.
# A giver's work (doc 83, Phase 2).
work_offer = There's work, fair price for it. | I've a job that pays. | Something wants doing, and I'll pay.
work_taken = Good trade. Don't dawdle. | Done deal, then. | Struck and settled.
work_paid = Here, fair price, as promised. | Paid to the penny. | Good trading. There you are.
work_back = No harm done. Another will buy it. | Fair enough. The deal's off, no cost. | Better unsold than spoiled.
work_none = Nothing on offer today. | No work going, friend. | Nothing worth the price just now.
# The other roles (doc 83, Phase 3).
inn_offer = Bed, board or a drink, fair priced? | Come in, customer. What'll it be? | A bed or a bite, friend?
inn_bed = Upstairs, and well worth the coin. There's a chest for your things. | Your bed's upstairs. Good trading tomorrow.
watch_greet = The watch. State your business, friend. | Keep the peace and we'll deal fairly.
church_greet = Bless you. Come in. | All are welcome here, friend.
learn_offer = It's a trade that pays its way. | It's honest work, fairly paid.
# The dialogue trees' other words (doc 83, Phase 4): news and gossip there isn't, a trouble that isn't, thanks for
# help, the way, a resident's work, work turned down, an inn's fire and its helpers, the watch's word, a master's
# new apprentice, something to hand over; a keeper asleep.
shop_asleep = Mm... stall's shut... no sales till morning. | Zzz... come back at market hours.
news_none = Nothing worth the price. | The market's quiet. | Not that I've heard, friend.
gossip_none = Market-mouths. Not me. | Nothing I'd sell you.
trouble_none = Nothing I can't haggle my way out of. | Nothing, friend.
trouble_thanks = That's worth more than silver. Thank you. | I owe you, friend. I won't forget it.
way_ask = Where to, friend? | Looking for a stall?
job_busy = Whatever pays. | A bit of trade, a bit of this.
work_declined = No sale, then. | Suit yourself, friend.
inn_rest = Sit by the fire. That's free; the bed upstairs isn't. | Take the bench. Nobody charges for sitting.
watch_noted = Noted. They'll pay for it. | Good. The watch thanks you, friend.
watch_bye = Keep the peace and trade fair. | Move along, friend.
learn_taken = Done. Be here when the stall opens, and stay near me. | Then it's a deal. Stay close while I work.
receiver_ask = Something for me? I've paid for nothing. | For me, friend?
inn_helper = I only help here. #here.innkeeper# sets the prices and lets the beds. | Ask #here.innkeeper#. I just carry the cups.
bless = Good fortune go with you | Bless you, and fair trading

# Discourse words (doc 84 §10.3, Phase 7): Larkfield's hedge, account, preclose, excuse and the rest (plain.grammar
# says what each is). Drafts, to be tuned by ear.
hedge = Hold the haggle a moment | Hear me out | Let's not haggle
account = I've my own business to mind | It wouldn't pay me
ack = Noted | Well, there's a thing to weigh
pivot = While we're bargaining. | That puts me in mind of another sale.
anyway = Anyway, that's another stall. | Enough haggling over that.
later = come next market | after the market closes
resume = Back to business | As I was pricing it
preclose = Well. Time's money | Still. Can't stand here haggling
excuse = Time's money | I've business to chase
knew = The market had it already | Heard it at the stalls
sympathy = A dear price, that | A bad bargain, that
concede = Your price, then | I'll grant you that, gratis
insist = I'll not drop my price | Mark my words, friend
warn = Count your change | Keep a paw on your purse in the crowd
welcome_back = Back for more bargains | Come back to haggle
welcome = Step up to the stall | Join the haggle
sorry = Knock it off the price | I spoke out of turn
forgive = No charge for it | Squared up, we are
deflect = No charge for that | On the house, that one
no_matter = No sale, no matter | Another market, then
go_ahead = Put it on the scales | Let's hear the offer
put_off = Not today; the scales are busy | Come back once the market's done
vent = Between us, off the books | Honest weight
reassure = It'll weigh out right | You'll make it back
congrats = A bargain well made | Full weight to you
demur = I'd not pay that price | Not at that weight
doubt = I'd weigh that twice | Sounds short-weight to me
listen = Listen, friend | Here's a thing

# Backchannel lines (doc 84 §10.2, Phase 7): plain's short answers ("Good.", "Always.", "It is.") as Larkfield says them.
# Drafts, to be tuned by ear; one left out falls back to plain's words.
bc_good = Full weight. | That's fair-priced.
bc_always = Every market day, without fail. | Regular as the scales.
bc_thank_you = Much obliged. | Ta, much obliged.
bc_it_is = It is, and no haggling. | That it is, full weight.
bc_ha = Ha! Sold. | Ha, I'd pay for that one.
bc_i_will = I will. Done. | Done, I will.
bc_very = Top-weight. | Very, top-weight.
bc_never = Never, not at any price. | Not for all the silver.
bc_probably = Even odds. | I'd lay a silver on it.
bc_we_will_see = We'll see at the weighing. | We'll see what the market says.
bc_maybe = Could be, at a price. | Maybe. Depends on the price.
bc_me_too = Same price for me. | And me. Same stall.
bc_it_does = It does, full weight. | That it does.
bc_again = Again, and no change given.
bc_lovely = Lovely, full weight. | A bargain, that.
bc_every_year = Every year, regular as market day. | Every year, same price.
bc_wise = Sharp. | Sharp trading, that.
bc_i_know = I know the price of it. | Don't I know it.
bc_so_they_say = So the market says. | So they're saying at the stalls.
bc_again_q = Again? At that price? | What, again?
bc_of_course = Of course. Done. | Course, at no charge.
bc_about_time = About time, and overdue. | Overdue, that.
bc_mine_too = Mine too, same price. | Same at my stall.
bc_ill_try = I'll try. No promises. | I'll give it a go.
bc_nor_me = Nor me, not at any price.
bc_they_do = They do, every market day. | That they do.
bc_poor_soul = Poor soul. Hard bargain, that. | Poor lamb.
bc_i_wont = I won't, on my scales. | Not a word, at any price.
bc_every_one = Every one, counted and weighed. | Every last one.
bc_not_me = Not me, not at any price. | Not this trader.
bc_very_good = Top-weight. | Full weight, that.
bc_go_on_then = Go on, then. Name your price. | Go on, then, done.
bc_barely = Barely, by a lark's worth. | Short weight, barely.
bc_lucky_you = A bargain for you, then. | Lucky you, at that price.
bc_every_night = Every night, after market. | Every night, regular as closing time.
bc_nobody = Not a soul at the stalls. | Nobody at all, not a customer.
bc_for_once = For once, a bargain.
bc_i_hope_so = I hope so, at that price. | Here's hoping.
bc_exactly = To the ounce. | Exact weight.
bc_everything = The whole stall. | Weights, scales and all.
bc_true = True weight. | Fair dealing, true.
bc_mostly = Mostly, give or take a penny. | Nine parts in ten.
bc_gladly = Gladly. Done. | Gladly, no charge.
bc_fair_enough = Fair enough. Done. | Fair enough, sold.
bc_every_time = Every time, regular as the scales. | Every sale.
bc_very_bad = Short weight, that. | Bad bargain.
bc_fair = Fair weight.
bc_bless_you = Bless your scales.
bc_always_is = Always is, every market. | Always is, same price.
bc_right = Right. Done. | Right you are.
bc_lucky = A bargain. | Lucky, that.
bc_bad_luck = Short-weighed luck. | Bad bargain, that.
bc_why_q = Why? What's the price of it? | Why? Where's the profit in that?
bc_true_enough = True enough. Done. | True weight.
bc_they_always_do = They always do, every market. | Always do.
bc_every_day = Every market day and between. | Every day, regular.
bc_very_busy = Rushed off my feet. | Busy as market day.
bc_im_sorry = I'm sorry. Full weight.
bc_so_do_i = So do I, same price. | And me, same stall.
bc_clever = Sharp. | Sharp trading.
bc_too_late = Too late. Sold already. | Too late, market's shut.
bc_go_on = Go on, I'm buying. | Go on, then. Out with the offer.
bc_we_will = We will. Done. | That we will, it's a deal.
bc_they_are = That they are, full weight. | They are, no haggling.
bc_so_am_i = So am I, same stall.
bc_let_them = Let them. Their loss. | Let them haggle.
bc_hope_so = Here's hoping. | Hope so, at that price.
bc_already = Already, and gone. | Already. Quick as a sale.
bc_i_might = I might, at the right price. | Might do.
bc_the_best = Top-weight. | Best in the market.
bc_somebody_has_to = Somebody has to mind the stall. | Somebody has to, no charge.
bc_nothing = Nothing worth a penny. | Nothing, not a penny's worth.
bc_and_q = And? What's the price? | And? Don't short-change me.
bc_who_q = Who's that, then?
bc_it_was = It was, full weight. | That it was.
